HOME COMMUNION Home Communion was delivered on August 13 to twelve members by five teams of elders and deacons. If you are unable to attend worship on Sunday, September 10 and would like to receive Holy Communion, please contact Jennie Alt at the church office. Many thanks to those who participate in bringing the Lord’s Supper.

ILLINOIS PRESBYTERIAN HOME

Dr. Kieffer and Rev. McGill will lead worship services every Thursday in September at 4:00 p.m. for the residents. WPC’s pastors lead worship in April and September annually.

PRAYER MINISTRY

We do not cease to give thanks for you as we remember you in our prayers. Ephesians 1:16

This September, Prayer Ministry at Westminster Presbyterian Church resumes. Throughout the year, each member of our congregation is remembered in prayer by the pastors. A letter is sent to members three weeks prior to the week they are being lifted up in prayer. We welcome prayer requests.

Dr. Angela Reed, author of Quest for Spiritual Community, talks about those who serve as “anchors” within faith communities by spending time in prayer. Your pastors consider it a privilege to gather weekly and to pray together on your behalf in the historic chancel of our beautiful Sanctuary.

You, too, can be one of Westminster’s anchors. We occasionally receive requests for prayers from the whole church. If you would like to be added to our email list of those who receive these requests and offer prayers on behalf of other church members, please contact the church office or talk to Rev. T rajan McGill.

FELLOWSHIP OPPORTUNITIES

KNIT 2 PURL 4 MISSION gather for fellowship and knitting every Tuesday at 1:00 p.m. in the Conference Room. Their next brown-bag lunch will be on September 12 beginning at 12:00 p.m. Dessert will be provided. If interested, contact Mary Jo Mattern Jenkins or Sue Cull.

DMA (DON’T MENTION AGE)
FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 15 - 6:30 P.M.
POTLUCK & 7:15 P.M. PROGRAM
We welcome Rev. Margaret Ann Jessup, Vice President of Associate Pastor of Douglas Avenue Methodist Church. She will present a program entitled “Wooden It Be Lovely.” Please come for the program, even if you are unable to attend the potluck and fellowship. Guests and youth are always welcome.

PRESBYTERIAN WOMEN
PW LUNCHEON will meet Friday, September 8 at noon in Parish Hall. Kitchen Chair will be Mary Ray and Chair of the Day will be Lynne O’Shea. The program will honor Compass Volunteers and be presented by Nancy Canady. Along with PW LUNCHEONS on the first Friday of each month at noon, women are able to sign up for the following fellowship circles:

ELIZABETH CIRCLE will meet Monday, September 11 at 5:30 p.m. in Second Floor Classroom. Lynne Cleverdon will lead our program “According to Paul” based on Chapter 5 of the PW Horizon Bible Study Who is Jesus? By Judy Yates Siker.

LYDIA CIRCLE resumes on Tuesday, September 19 at 9:30 a.m. at the church in the Library. Sharon Zook will be the hostess and Sharon Turner will give devotions. We will study Lesson 1: Mark of the PW Horizon Bible Study Who is Jesus? By Judy Yates Siker.

RUTH AND NAOMI CIRCLE meets the first Wednesday at 7:00 p.m. beginning October 4 at the home of Pat Wheat (11 Pickering Lane 62712). We will discuss the Introduction and Chapter 1 of Judith Viorst’s book “Imperfect Control: Our Lifelong Struggles with Power and Surrender”

PRESBYTERIAN MEN
MONTHLY MEN’S BREAKFAST resumes on Thursday, September 14, at 7:00 a.m. in Parish Hall. Come join us for food and fellowship on the second Thursday of each month. September’s breakfast will feature a program on Genealogy presented by Todd Knox. David Mitchell will lead a devotion, and Pete VanGieson will provide breakfast for the group. Please call the church office to let us know you plan to be there, so we can be sure to have enough food.

WORLDWIDE PHOTO WALK ON SATURDAY, OCTOBER 7 – Interested in photography? Member Bob Fox will lead a walking tour in the area of Lake Springfield beginning at the Henson Robinson Zoo, on the morning of Saturday, October 7. Join the group in looking for opportunities for scenic and nature photographs, and enjoy sharing and getting tips from other participants on taking good shots. The Worldwide Photo walk supports The Springs of Hope Kenya Orphanage, an organization that feeds, houses, educates and empowers young orphans. Contact Bob Fox at email@example.com, or go to http://worldwidephotowalk.com, pick Springfield, IL as the location and sign up.

