1.0 INTRODUCTION:

In November 1991, a group of twenty people, from different parts of Queensland came together in Brisbane. They met over two days to review their collective experience of being involved in transition programs within disability service organisations. The experience of the participants included both accommodation and family support services for children as well as accommodation, residential support and employment services for adults. The majority of participants, at the time of the workshop, held positions as managers of transition projects. All projects had received 'transition' funding through the Commonwealth Department of Health, Housing and Community Services.

The Community Resource Unit Inc, who sponsored the two day event has for some time been concerned about the lack of attention, at various levels, given to the process of effecting qualitative change in disability services. Many attempts at change appear to:

* be amazingly naive;

* lack a practical understanding of the actual process and dynamics of change;

* grossly underestimate the complexity and difficulty of the task at hand; and

* have a low degree of consciousness of the risks for people and consequently have insufficient safeguards built into the change program.

As a result of CRU'S work within the field in Queensland we have concluded that achieving worthwhile change is neither simple nor unproblematic. The experience of recent years in attempting to bring about change in services, that purportedly exist to serve people with disabilities, provides many lessons for the intending change agent as well as for governments that are responsible for policy and funding.

1.1 The Purpose of this Paper:

It is hoped that this paper will contribute to the discussion and debate about change strategies for disability services. It offers the insight and experience gained in recent years, by a number of practitioners and consultants working in the Queensland disability field. A number of the group participants have also worked outside Queensland in other States of Australia, as well as overseas. In addition it also offers the wisdom of leading thinkers and writers and attempts to provide an integrative perspective by matching the state of general knowledge of planned organisational change with experiences of concrete change efforts.

The Brisbane workshop attempted to provide a bridge between practice and theory in the field of organisational change. However, there was insufficient time at the workshop to complete a comprehensive exploration of useful theories of change. That shortcoming will be addressed in this document.

1.2 Structure of the Paper:

The paper will be divided into three main sections with a summary of key findings included at the beginning to provide easy reference for the reader. The summary will give:

(i) a list of conditions that were identified as being necessary in the effective management of change; as well as

(ii) a discussion of the major lessons learnt through participation in transition programs.

Part A of this paper will deal with key issues and challenges to do with effecting change in disability programs. It will endeavour to integrate the reflection on the first-hand experience of participants with some current thinking about change.

Part B will give an overview of the Brisbane workshop and will include the results of the work undertaken during the course of the workshop.

Part C provides a description and some discussion of a number of models of change which may prove helpful as an introduction to the task of planning for change. References are given to enable the serious change agent to research more deeply.

The content will be of interest and relevance to service providers, internal and external change agents, as well as State and Commonwealth Government Departments. In addition, consumers, families, and advocates should find the content informative and perhaps useful should they become actively involved in change processes as participating stakeholders.

For organisations which are only interested in making superficial changes to comply with the 'letter' of the legislation, and who are fundamentally not in tune with the 'spirit' of the reforms, the content and thrust of this paper will have little relevance.

1.3 Limitations of the Report:

It must be stressed that this particular paper largely focuses on the possibilities of change within services for people with disabilities and their families. This should not be interpreted to mean that service redevelopment or transformation is being offered as the answer or solution to the problem of how to significantly enrich the lives of people with disabilities. A vision of a valued life for people with disabilities might include transformed formal support systems, but it is just as likely to include the 'non-service' relationships and experiences. Such are difficult to quantify but are generally characterised by the qualities of acceptance, belonging, participation, friendship, interdependency, respect, dignity and intimacy.

In change programmes it's useful to know what you're aiming at!

2.0 SUMMARY

2.1 SUMMARY OF KEY ISSUES

Achieving change in human service systems is always likely to be problematic... It appears that agencies will always find it easier to carry out functions with which they are familiar and which do not inconvenience them to any great extent. Wolfensberger reminds us that service structures have built-in resistances to the change process. This fact seems to be difficult for many service executives and administrators to accept. Lip service is frequently paid to change by those who usually sincerely believe that they are committed to innovation and adaptation. (Wolfensberger, 1977)

Yet ultimately,........, service delivery agencies are essentially quasi bureaucracies which require a great deal of continuity and routine in their operation in order to function relatively efficiently. Change and continuity are inherently opposed to each other, and while too much change would result in chaos, there are extremely few human services agencies that change as much as they should.

Wolfensberger, (1977, p.1-2)

What is fundamentally required to assist people with disabilities who are dependent upon service agencies to have 'better lives' is visionary leadership that demonstrates a positive, respectful and enduring commitment to the people they serve. It requires a commitment that remains even if it is inconvenient and breaks organisational continuity.

In addition there is the issue of leadership competence to facilitate change. This relates to the requirement to ensure that there is an application of valid and appropriate knowledge and change agentry skills in services, which will inform a conscious, deliberate and collaborative effort to improve services for people with disabilities. An extremely important part of leadership competence is the awareness of the significance of organisational culture.

"People do not change in a vacuum. They change in social environments which they have helped to create. Any perceived threat to the existence/identity of their group can be ferociously defended."

Generally, the need for change can be rationally presented, but if the proposed change is perceived as an attack or a threat it can frequently produce the unleashing of strong non-rational or irrational forces. All resistance to change is not negative and change agents require a capacity to enter the reality of the people whose lives are going to be affected. Good listening skills are a start.

A major lesson coming out of the Queensland experience is the necessity of involving key stakeholders in the change process. Empirical research has also borne this out. Participation reduces resistance and facilitates a sharing of the ownership of change. The legitimate interests of parents should be acknowledged. In recent years many parents have been left behind in the dust as others have steered and controlled the change process.

Their involvement in many cases has been minimal. Sufficient time has not been taken to explain and discuss the rationale for the change. Working with parents in small numbers and in smaller situations offers greater promise and potential. Our service systems must recognise that the commitment of parents to individual sons or daughters is usually long term and outlasts the transitory interests of most professionals.

Planning and effecting change is both an intellectual and a practical task. It does also contain a moral challenge to those who would improve the quality of life of citizens with disabilities. Given the vulnerability of devalued people during periods of change and uncertainty, innovations should only be embarked upon after due consideration of the risks and the development of safeguards.

"In this sense real qualitative change in the lives of people with disabilities has both a progressive and a conservative dimension."

The people costs of change can be high. The ever present danger is that change agents can be spurred on by the attractiveness of the current service 'answer' or 'fashion'. Believing that this or that particular service model is what is required, people can be 'seduced' into committing to ideas or technologies rather than to human beings.

Very little measure has been taken of the personal costs, consequences and implications of large scale change programs for people with disabilities and their families. As well as being hurtful to consumers and parents, service transitions can also be hurtful to front line change workers. They often feel alienated and if their actions are perceived to be a threat to powerful vested interests, they may become ostracised within the organisation.

Unsuccessful change efforts can hurt people

2.2 Conditions For Effective Management of Change in Human Services

* The involvement of key stakeholders as participants in the overall change process.

* The recognition of the legitimate interests of parents and families (as key stakeholders). Their involvement in the change process is to be sought and supported.

* The creation of a vision of future valued outcomes for those persons with a disability that the organisation serves. Plus a vision of what a service organisation may need to do to deliver a quality service.

* A strong sense of purpose or 'mission' for the organisation. A clear idea of why the organisation exists. This must be embedded in all the layers of the organisation, staff, management, and consumers.

* A leadership which is aware of the vulnerabilities of people with disabilities and accepts the moral responsibility attached to giving service.

* A 'people first' rather than a 'structure first' approach. Awareness of the 'people effects' of change strategies.

* An understanding of the challenge, complexity and nature of change processes.

* Acknowledgment of the centrality/fundamentality of values in an organisation.

* An understanding of the culture of the organisation and what it takes to re-shape it. Knowing the history, the mythology and traditions of the organisation and the importance of linking strategies to its culture.

* A staff who are clear about their role in the organisation and what part they play in fulfilling the organisation's mission and vision.

* Anticipation of resistance and a planned approach for managing it.

* Recognition of the importance of 'modelling' - leading by example and personal commitment.

* Reward systems that reflect values and priorities.

* Effective communication throughout all levels of the organisation.

* A preparedness to 'labour in the trenches' and to be suspicious of quick fix-it solutions - quality change usually takes time.

* A clear sense of the organisation's interdependency with its outside environment. An understanding that parts of the environment will constantly affect it. For example cultural attitudes and assumptions, the demands of government, political pressure etc.

* A mechanism for managing and monitoring progress and for balancing stability and change.

* The support and co-operation of senior management.

* The input of an external adviser to support and guide the internal change efforts.

* Effective goal setting techniques. Communication of clear and reachable scenarios.

2.3 Lessons Learned:

The major lessons learned through the experience of attempting to implement change in service systems are included here. Much of the discussion amplifies the points raised in the previous sections (2.1 & 2.2).

Key Areas:

PARTICIPATION

SHARED OWNERSHIP OF CHANGE

UNDERSTANDING THE PERSPECTIVES OF STAKEHOLDERS

LEADERSHIP

VISION

COMMUNICATION AND THE FLOW OF INFORMATION

TIME-FRAMES

ORGANISATIONAL CULTURE

PERSONAL STYLE OF CHANGE AGENT

NEEDS OF THE CHANGE AGENT

VALUES BASE

WISE DECISION MAKING

WORKING WITH PARENTS

RESISTANCE TO CHANGE

PREDICTING CHANGE POTENTIAL

Participation:

It is important to make the change process participative. If there are shared goals it is more likely that people will move forward together. Participation can commence in the early stages, even before transition formally starts. It is important to include as many stakeholders as possible but especially clients or consumers and families.

It may be necessary to put in place supports to allow for participation, e.g. augmentative communication systems, external facilitators, and mechanisms for giving feedback. The issue of communication with people with severe communication impairments was recognized as being an important area that should receive more attention.

Stakeholder participation can be both formal and informal. Collaborative participation is more effective in getting agreement about change.

Participation of key personnel in planning and problem solving is crucial. Strategic planning exercises should involve significant people from different levels of the organisation.

**Shared Ownership of Change**

Change occurs best when all levels of the organisation want it and share the ownership of the ideas and hopes for the future. Effective participation of key stakeholders leads to shared ownership of the agenda for change.

The transition message needs to be legitimated through shared ownership of the ideas and philosophy underpinning the proposed change. If the thrust for change is only coming from one or two staff members who are accountable to a more senior officer then the potential for change is not high. Likewise if it is consumers or parents wanting change but do not have the support of other levels in the organisation it is very difficult for them to make any significant impact.

"Getting Commitment from the Board (Committee of Management) is vital if real change is to be achieved."

**Understanding the Perspectives of Stakeholders**

Most changes in service systems will have an impact on those who have a direct interest or 'stake' in the service. How stakeholders perceive the personal impact of proposed changes for them, will significantly influence the success or failure of the change process. It is necessary to examine who the proposed change will threaten and why. This will help to create the means of addressing stakeholder concerns.

"It is important to understand that people commit to a course of action for their own reasons, not for the change agent's reasons."

Find out what is important to stakeholders - what gives satisfaction in the current situation and how can this be increased? People are unlikely to co-operate if they see changes as likely to decrease their present level of satisfaction.

Change agents need to focus on the unique needs of different stakeholder groups. For example staff have a very different interest in the service compared with service users. Different strategies may be required for different stakeholders.

**Leadership:**

It was agreed that one of the most important factors in change programs is leadership. The initial desire and energy for change may arise externally or internally, perhaps at a middle management level. Any attempt to influence the behaviour of other people may be described as leadership.

"Leadership does not always equate with status or formal authority within an organisation. Nevertheless the active commitment of the organisation's senior managers to change is a critical factor."

Ideally there should be on-going official support and endorsement for the transition program.

Leaders need to communicate values and direction with inspiration and clarity. The organisational leadership must communicate a vision for the future and then build an organisational culture which is conducive to achieving the vision. A leader will need to do this through giving a consistent message about the underpinning values and the desired outcomes.

The leader will also need to demonstrate, in the making of day to day organisational decisions, the significance and priority of change efforts. The strategic leader will use human resource functions of recruitment, promotion, rewards or dismissal, to support the organisational vision and goals.

Vision:

It was shown to be very difficult to get the co-operation of stakeholders if there was not a clear vision of the future, or the outcomes, that 'transition' was leading towards. The shaping of the vision for the organisation was best achieved through the involvement and collaboration of key personnel from different levels of the organisation.

"However describing a vision for the future of individual consumers must involve the person themselves and the key people in their lives."

Communication and the Flow of Information:

It is important to ensure that there is effective and open communication throughout the organisation. There needs to be a climate that supports open communication. Otherwise information, critical for the success of the project, may not be given. Information blocks must be dealt with. Change agents need to understand the way in which information tends to move most easily through the organisation and capitalise on the process.

"Staff charged with new goals and responsibilities require information, support, encouragement and regular feedback as they implement the plan."

Time Frames:

Short time frames make it difficult to make real change. If time is limited it makes sense to aim for small changes.

The choice of a time frame should be determined by the particular situation. The speed of change should be determined by the risks, resistance to change, impact on people's lives, and what the solution might be.

In some situations where gross neglect may be occurring the speed of change, of necessity must be as fast as possible. In most cases it makes sense to move more slowly, taking a step by step approach.

Organisational Culture:

The failure of change agents to understand the significance of organisational culture was a severe deficit for people working to effect change in organisations. It is essential to become acquainted with the culture of the organisation, to build on it, reshape where necessary so it will reinforce the mission and the goals of the organisation.

Personal Style of Change Agent:

Important characteristics and actions were:

- Being diplomatically assertive;
- Understanding where people are coming from;
- Identifying what people's fears may be;
- Respecting the ideas of others;
- During consultations make sure that people who aren't usually heard are listened to;
- Develop good listening skills.

Needs of the Change Agent:

The change agent must believe in the need for change. Be clear about your motives. Have a strong vision, for e.g. a desire to see people with disabilities experiencing acceptance, having opportunities for development and enjoying quality lifestyles. He/she must be dissatisfied with the current situation and be committed to making better lives a reality.

It is not enough to just know about the subject matter of service/types or models and individualised planning techniques for consumers.

"Knowledge about the change process itself is required."

Understand what your role is, and identify where you are in the power structure. Understand the limits of your authority and influence. But know who are the key people to influence in favour of the proposed changes.

Know who you can rely on. Have a network around you who keep you grounded. These may be workers, parents, consumers. Encourage them to challenge you as well as support you.

Be modest and cautious. Critique your ideas. Be prepared for challenges and frustrations. Be wary of the 'quick fix' approach.

**Value Base:**

It is important to rest changes on a strong value base. Values, consciously or unconsciously, impact on attitudes and behaviour of individuals within organisations. Values, and the assumptions that stem from them, can unite or divide people.

"Senior management should, as far as possible, articulate a value base for services. Staff should be recruited, trained and supported on the basis of these values or beliefs."

The values that are significant are those to do with what is believed to be desirable and possible for people with disabilities given the particular focus or responsibility of the service.

Values and assumptions will underpin the organisation's vision for the future, both for individual consumers and for the organisation.

**Wise Decision Making:**

Weigh up the forces for change.
What's the balance? When is the battle too uphill? What are the personal costs?
Examine resources against goals.
Choose where to invest energies wisely.

**Working with Parents:**

The importance of parents as stakeholders should be recognized. They require an investment of time and information.

Empower parents, using outside sources. Hearing about changes can be more powerful when it comes from someone who's had similar experiences and fears, and has found the benefits of change.

Recognize their power and how they can support and educate each other, and how they can be a powerful lobby group.

In the early stages it works best to speak with parents individually rather than in groups.

Differences between transition workers and senior management, in relation to the contribution of parents, can present difficulties when it comes to determining strategies for participation and collaboration. Workers may be trying to work closely with parents while the existing culture is characterised by judgemental attitudes towards parents.

Resistance:

It is important to understand resistance and to expect it. Effecting change is hard, complicated work. The difficulty arises, in part, because the defenders of the status quo have the advantage if they do not become part of the change effort. They have history, tradition and established practices on their side.

Resistance should be assumed and planned for. Resistance takes different forms. It should be recognized and strategies used for preventing, reducing and dealing with it.

Resistance to change is inevitable, but we must acknowledge that not all resistance is external. We have all (change agents included) grown up in a culture that devalues people with disabilities and are as likely to confront prejudice within ourselves as among service workers or fellow citizens.

Predicting Change Potential:

External consultants had learned that the presence of key people within organisations, who a) stood in support of people with disabilities and were committed to working in their best interests; and b) were committed and willing to invest time and energy, were important factors in predicting the potential for organisational change. Without such individuals or groups, consultancy intervention is likely to have little impact of an enduring nature.

