This framework is comprehensive in its outline as it attempts to include, for planning purposes, all the important areas/issues that need to be addressed in a major human service change project. Some examples of the kind of projects for which it may provide a useful guide are:

* development of community based accommodation and support options for people previously institutionalised;

* a change in program content from custodial day centre to a range of vocational, further educational, recreational activities;

* development of a community residential support service to replace an existing group home model;

* development of a family support service as an alternative to residential placement for children with disabilities;

* creation of an individualised holiday service as an alternative to traditional centre-based respite; and

* a change from a sheltered workshop program to an individualised program to assist people to find and keep a job.

The framework is intended for seriously minded change agents and organisations who are fundamentally at odds with the dominant values and ideology of society and the traditional human service system.

Its content has been informed by:-

* the lessons that have been learned in the past ten or so years;

* some insightful theoretical and practical work done by others; and,

* (at a most fundamental level by) social role valorisation principles (Wolfensberger 1983).

It is not possible within the confines of this paper to fully amplify each and every dimension of the framework. A good deal of relevant detail may be gleaned from other sections of this paper. It is simply a planning tool, another piece of technology which may or may not be helpful. It is certainly not presented as 'the only way to do it.'

What is fundamental to this particular approach is the recognition of the primary and over-riding importance of the role that assumptions, and their underlying values, play in what is planned and made available to people with disabilities.

Value-statements about the worth and uniqueness of persons with a disability can be easily espoused. In Annual Reports, mission statements and lists of principles and objectives they often read like platitudes. Our rhetoric is not echoed in our lives, societally or personally. Frequently however the basic assumptions that people hold about people with disabilities, at an unconscious level, will continue to direct and limit what actually happens for them. For the most part the assumptions held about people with disabilities in our society are false. Mistaken beliefs about the differentness, competence, potential, intentions and worth of people with disabilities are revealed in the actions and behaviour of others towards them. Rejection, being assigned a low status, extremely limited access to valued social roles, stigmatisation, segregation, congregation, impoverishment and brutalisation are common life experiences of people with disabilities.

The starting point for Funnell's approach is the checking out of basis assumptions about people with disabilities, about the role of human services, in order to bring the important value questions into consciousness where they can be challenged and clarified. Unless the value issues are addressed, a change program designed to improve life conditions for people with disabilities is likely to end up looking a lot like the original program that was targeted for change.

There can be a number of forces for change and not all of them will be driven by assumptions and values that are enhancing of the dignity of people with disabilities. A commitment to change 'for the right reasons' is the first and most fundamental decision to be taken.

Within this framework for planning for change, what follows, although largely technical in nature, should also be based on clear values and a positive commitment to people with disabilities and their allies and a determination to do, as far as possible, what is necessary to achieve the best outcomes for them.

Because of the uncertainty and anxiety that frequently accompanies change it is important, as an expression of a commitment to stand alongside vulnerable people, to design safeguards which will reduce the likelihood of things going wrong. Two very important safeguards, not specifically itemised in the framework are- the involvement of independent advocates, and the submission to external comment and critique. Since failures in planning and implementation are always probable, there should be further safeguards in the form contingency plans.

